A solid, optimal solution for Max Number of K-Sum Pairs uses the Two-Pointer Technique.

The solution you are looking at on the screen is actually the standard optimal approach for this problem when you want to keep space complexity at a minimum. It works by sorting the array first, which allows you to systematically narrow down pairs from both ends.

Here is a clean implementation using standard for and while loops, breaking down exactly why it works.

💻 Optimal Two-Pointer Solution
JavaScript
var maxOperations = function(nums, k) {
    // 1. Sort the array in ascending order
    nums.sort(function(a, b) { 
        return a - b; 
    });
    
    let count = 0;
    let i = 0;
    let j = nums.length - 1;
    
    // 2. Meet in the middle
    while (i < j) {
        let sum = nums[i] + nums[j];
        
        if (sum === k) {
            count++;
            i++;
            j--;
        } else if (sum > k) {
            // Sum is too large, move the right pointer left to find a smaller number
            j--;
        } else {
            // Sum is too small, move the left pointer right to find a larger number
            i++;
        }
    }
    
    return count;
};
🧠 Why This Approach Works
By sorting the array, you gain predictability. If your current sum is too big, you know for a fact that moving the right pointer (j) to the left will decrease the sum. If the sum is too small, moving the left pointer (i) to the right will increase it.

Time Complexity: O(nlogn) because of the initial sorting step. The subsequent while loop only takes O(n) time since each element is visited at most once.

Space Complexity: O(1) (or O(n) depending on the engine's internal sorting implementation), but it avoids allocating an entirely new hash map data structure in memory.

🔄 Alternative: The O(n) Hash Map Approach
If you ever need to optimize for Time Complexity over space, you can use a Hash Map (Frequency Counter) to track available numbers. This brings the time complexity down to O(n) but increases the space complexity to O(n) to store the frequencies. For an interview or a strict performance test, the Two-Pointer approach above is usually preferred by interviewers because of its O(1) auxiliary space efficiency.

do the hash map approach
The Hash Map (Frequency Counter) approach is the way to go if you want to optimize for Time Complexity. Instead of sorting the array, you can trade a little bit of memory to find pairs in a single pass.

Here is the clean, standard implementation using standard for loops and an object as a hash map.

💻 Hash Map Solution
JavaScript
var maxOperations = function(nums, k) {
    let map = {};
    let count = 0;
    
    for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        let current = nums[i];
        let complement = k - current;
        
        // If the complement exists and has a count greater than 0, we found a pair
        if (map[complement] > 0) {
            count++;
            map[complement]--; // Use up one instance of the complement
        } else {
            // Otherwise, store the current number's frequency in the map
            if (map[current] === undefined) {
                map[current] = 1;
            } else {
                map[current]++;
            }
        }
    }
    
    return count;
};
🧠 Why This Works
Instead of looking ahead or sorting to find a match, you iterate through the array and look backward at what you've already seen:

For every number (current), you calculate its target partner: complement = k - current.

You check your tracking object (map) to see if that complement is sitting there waiting to be paired.

If it is available, you match them up, increment your total operations, and decrement its availability so it can't be reused.

If it's not available, you log the current number into the map so a future number can potentially pair up with it.

📈 Complexity Analysis
Time Complexity: O(n). You only loop through the nums array exactly once. Looking up and updating properties in a JavaScript object takes O(1) average time, making this significantly faster than sorting for large datasets.

Space Complexity: O(n). In the worst-case scenario (e.g., no pairs are found at all), you will end up storing every single element of the array inside the hash map.

